---
/**
 * An entry's header — the eyebrow, the title, the permalink, and the optional
 * full-bleed hero beneath them.
 *
 * ── EXTRACTED, NOT WRITTEN (2026-09-02) ──────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * Every line of this file already existed: the markup and CSS come from the article
 * page, and the note entry had independently written the title half. Case studies
 * would have been the THIRD title band and the SECOND hero treatment, which is well
 * past DESIGN.md's promotion trigger, so it moved here before that copy was made
 * rather than after.
 *
 * The move is meant to be behaviour-neutral and was verified as such — see the note
 * at the end of this comment.
 *
 * ── THE ONE THING THAT DIFFERS BETWEEN CONSUMERS, AND IT IS DERIVED ──────────
 *
 * The gap below the title. It is 16 when the title is bound to the prose that
 * follows it, the way a heading is bound to its paragraph, and a full band gap when
 * something sits between them. What sits between them is THE HERO — so:
 *
 *   no hero      the title is always adjacent to the prose   ->  16 at every width
 *   with a hero  adjacent only at lg, where the hero moves   ->  band gap, 16 at lg
 *                above the title
 *
 * That is one rule seen twice, not two rules, which is why `data-hero` derives it
 * instead of a `titleGap` prop. The note page reached 16-at-every-width by its own
 * reasoning and this reproduces it exactly.
 *
 * MOBILE-FIRST HOLDS THROUGH THE DERIVATION. The base case is the no-hero value,
 * and a hero page below lg takes the band gap — which is the phone layout, where the
 * hero really is between the title and the prose. An engine that never evaluates the
 * lg query is left with something correct rather than with a desktop remnant.
 *
 * ── THE FLEX COLUMN LIVES HERE NOW ───────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * It was on the article page's `.entry` (the <article> element). It moved down with
 * the reorder, which is the correct home rather than an accident of nesting: flex
 * earns its place ONLY because of `order`, and the two items that reorder are the
 * title band and the hero — both children of this component. The <article> above it
 * stacks its children in ordinary block flow, which produces an identical result
 * with no machinery.
 *
 * Bands stay full bleed for free: the cross axis of a column flex container is
 * horizontal, and `align-items` defaults to `stretch`.
 *
 * ── WHAT THE MOVE ACTUALLY CHANGED, AND WHAT IT DID NOT ──────────────────────
 *
 * Not byte-identical output, which is what an extraction usually aims for — so it is
 * worth writing down exactly what moved. Enumerated across all 47 built pages by
 * diffing the normalised HTML and collecting every distinct changed token; there were
 * only these:
 *
 *   1. `<article class="entry h-entry">` -> `<article class="h-entry">`  (42 pages)
 *   2. a `<div class="entry-header">` wrapper appears, with `data-hero` on 42 of the
 *      45 that have one — the 3 without are the notes
 *   3. the inline <style> blocks come out in a DIFFERENT ORDER
 *
 * (1) and (2) are the intended cost: the flex column moved down a level, so it needs
 * an element to sit on.
 *
 * (3) WAS NOT INTENDED AND IS WORTH KNOWING. Astro emits component styles in an order
 * derived from the import graph, and moving `SanityHero`'s import out of the article
 * page and into this file reordered it — `Figure`'s rules now precede the hero's. The
 * RULE SET is unchanged: 24 rules before, 24 after, identical content. Only the
 * sequence differs.
 *
 * That is benign here but not benign in general, because sequence decides ties. Every
 * rule is scoped by its own `data-astro-cid`, so two components' rules can only meet
 * on one element through the cid pass-down — a parent styling a child's root — and
 * then only conflict if they set the same property. **So an extraction that moves an
 * import can silently reorder the cascade.** Checked by measurement rather than by
 * reasoning: at 1280 and at 375, on both consumers, every gap this file owns matches
 * what was measured before the move — 8 eyebrow-to-h1, 16 or 32 below the title per
 * the rule above, a 320 hero, an out-of-flow caption at lg and an in-flow one below,
 * and the rail's top still level with the body's.
 */
import type {SanityImageSource} from '../sanity/image'
import Band from './Band.astro'
import Grid from './Grid.astro'
import Eyebrow from './Eyebrow.astro'
import SanityHero from './SanityHero.astro'

interface Props {
  title?: string | null
  genre?: string | null
  /** ISO date string, straight from `pubDate`. */
  date?: string | null
  /** The canonical permalink, for `u-url` / `u-uid`. Built by the page from `Astro.site`. */
  permalink: string
  /**
   * The index this entry belongs to — `/insights/` or `/presentations/` — passed
   * straight through to <Eyebrow>, whose genre link this header always enables.
   *
   * Required, and threaded rather than defaulted: the base was hardcoded inside Eyebrow
   * and the presentation detail page inherited Insights' without anything failing
   * (Andy, 2026-09-09). Every consumer now states which collection it is part of.
   */
  basePath: string
  /**
   * The entry's hero. Absent on a note, present on an article or case study — and its
   * presence is what selects the title gap above, so this is a layout input and not
   * only content.
   */
  hero?: (SanityImageSource & {caption?: string | null}) | null
}

/*
 * ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
 * │  THIS COMPONENT'S PROPS ARE NOT CHECKED AT ITS CALL SITES. MEASURED, NOT     │
 * │  GUESSED — AND NOT YET EXPLAINED. Do not trust `astro check` to catch a      │
 * │  missing or mistyped prop here.                                              │
 * └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
 *
 * Found on 2026-09-09 while fixing the `/insights/` hardcode below. Against a page
 * calling <EntryHeader>, all three of these passed `astro check` CLEAN:
 *
 *   omitting `permalink`, required since this file was written
 *   `permalink={12345}`, where the type is string
 *   an invented `bogusProp={true}`
 *
 * A positive control proves the file is being read: an undefined identifier inside the
 * same tag raises ts(2304). So EXPRESSIONS in prop values are checked and the PROPS
 * THEMSELVES are not — which is how a wrong `basePath` could have shipped as silently
 * as the hardcode it replaced.
 *
 * ── IT IS NOT THE DESTRUCTURING STYLE, WHICH WAS THE OBVIOUS SUSPECT ─────────
 *
 * <TopicList> IS enforced — dropping its `basePath` raises ts(2322) naming the missing
 * property — and it differs from this file in using `: Props = Astro.props`. But
 * switching this file to the annotation only moved the error inside: `Astro.props` is
 * typed `Record<string, any>` HERE and `Props` in TopicList, so the annotation fails to
 * assign while call sites stay unchecked either way. Both forms were measured.
 *
 * So the real fault is that Astro is not associating this file's `Props` interface with
 * the component at all, and the reason for that is NOT yet known. The assertion is kept
 * because it is what the three sibling components do (CaseStudyEntry, NoteEntry,
 * GenreNav — all likely unenforced for the same reason) and because the annotation buys
 * nothing while erroring.
 *
 * Worth chasing separately: 26 components use the annotation form and are presumably
 * fine, so this is a minority, but a silently unenforced prop contract is exactly the
 * gap that let this file's bug through.
 *
 * ── WHAT SAVES THE `basePath` CHAIN ANYWAY, AND IT IS NOT THIS FILE ──────────
 *
 * Both LEAF components are enforced — measured: <Eyebrow>'s `genreLink`/`basePath`
 * union raises ts(2322) naming the branch, and <TopicList>'s required `basePath` does
 * the same. This file is only the pass-through.
 *
 * And if a future page does forget it here, the failure is safe rather than silent:
 * `basePath` arrives `undefined`, <Eyebrow>'s `genre && basePath` guard yields no href,
 * and the genre renders as PLAIN TEXT. A missing link is visible and harmless; a link
 * to the wrong collection is neither, and that is the bug being fixed.
 */
const {title, genre, date, permalink, basePath, hero} = Astro.props as Props

/* `hero.asset` and not `hero`, matching the article page's own guard: the field is an
   object on every document that has the field at all, so only the asset says whether
   there is an image. */
const hasHero = Boolean(hero?.asset)
---

<div class="entry-header" data-hero={hasHero ? '' : undefined}>
  {
    /*
    <header>, not Band's `section` default and not a <div>. Inside an <article>,
    <header> is precisely "introductory or navigational aids for that article",
    which is what an eyebrow and a title are. A <section> would claim a thematic
    grouping and put a nameless entry in the document outline; <div> would say
    nothing.

    AND IT IS NOT A SECOND `banner`. Because its nearest sectioning ancestor is
    <article>, it maps to `sectionheader` — a real role added in ARIA 1.3, which
    replaced the older `generic` mapping for a scoped <header>. So the masthead
    stays the page's only banner, and this carries meaning rather than nothing.
    Confirmed in Chrome's accessibility pane, 2026-08-26: `article > sectionheader`.

    The same scoping rule lands the other way for the site footer, which sits
    outside <main> and so maps to `contentinfo`. Moving a <footer> inside an
    <article> or <main> silently demotes it to `sectionfooter` — worth knowing,
    because it looks like a harmless move.

    `space="none"` with the padding on the inner <Grid> — the Footer's precedent,
    and not a style preference. `.band[data-space='band']` is (0,2,0) and a
    consumer's `.title-band` is (0,1,0), so the padding cannot be overridden from
    outside without escalating; putting it on the Grid, which sets no
    `padding-block` at all, sidesteps the fight entirely.
  */
  }
  <Band as="header" space="none" class="title-band">
    <Grid class="title-grid">
      <div class="title-block">
        {
          /* `microformats` and `genreLink` are always on, because this component only
             ever renders a detail page's own entry — which is exactly the context
             where `dt-published` is meaningful and where a genre link has room to
             live. Both are opt-in on Eyebrow for the CARDS' sake, not for this. */
        }
        <Eyebrow genre={genre} date={date} microformats genreLink basePath={basePath} />
        <h1 class="p-name">{title}</h1>

        {
          /*
          ── THE PERMALINK, AND WHY IT IS A <data> ──────────────────────────

          `u-url` is how a webmention receiver knows WHICH post a mention
          targets, and what a syndicated copy links back to — so it is arguably
          the most important property after name and content. It is also the one
          with nothing on the page to hang on: there is no visible self-link,
          and the implied-url rule does not fire, since that only applies when
          the root contains exactly one `a[href]` and an article contains many.

          `data[value]` is the mf2 parsing spec's own hook for a `u-*` with no
          visible anchor, alongside `a[href]` and `img[src]`. It is valid flow
          content anywhere, it renders nothing, and being empty it puts nothing
          in the accessibility tree. A `<link rel>` here would NOT be valid —
          `<link>` is metadata content and is only permitted in <body> when it
          carries `itemprop`.

          The alternative worth knowing is the classic blog pattern where the
          DATE is the permalink link. That would make this visible markup rather
          than invisible, at the cost of a link from a page to itself.

          `u-uid` alongside it, on the same value: `u-url` says "where this
          lives", `u-uid` says "this is its identity", and a consumer seeing the
          same post at two URLs uses the uid to deduplicate. For a static site
          with one canonical path they are the same string, and saying so is
          cheaper than leaving the identity implicit.

          PREVIEW BUILDS EMIT THE PREVIEW DOMAIN, because `Astro.site` differs
          by build mode. That is correct rather than a bug — preview is
          `noindex` and nothing syndicates from it — but it does mean this value
          is not a stable identifier across the two environments.
        */
        }
        <data class="u-url u-uid" value={permalink}></data>
      </div>
    </Grid>
  </Band>

  {
    /*
    THE IMAGE IS OUTSIDE <Grid>, deliberately — a grid would inset it to the
    content columns and the full bleed would quietly disappear. SanityHero's own
    header says so; this is the call site that has to honor it.

    THE CAPTION IS NOT IN A <Grid> EITHER — it aligns to the VIEWPORT edge, not
    the content column, matching the live site (Andy, 2026-08-26). A <figure> is an
    ordinary full-width block, so wrapping costs the bleed nothing, and the caption
    right-aligns inside it.

    An earlier draft put the caption in its own <Grid> at `--col-main`, on the
    reasoning that a caption is reading matter and belongs on the text measure.
    That is the wrong model for this one: it is a photo credit hanging off the
    corner of a full-bleed image, not prose.

    19 of 42 heroes carry a caption and several are photo credits ("Photo by
    Devin Avery on Unsplash"), so this is an attribution obligation rather than a
    nicety. 6 of the 7 case studies carry one too. The Figma Article board draws
    the hero as 320px of image and nothing else, which is why it was left out of
    the first draft; Andy's call, 2026-08-26.
  */
  }
  {
    hasHero && hero && (
      <figure class="hero-figure">
        {/*
          `u-photo` reads the `src`, so it belongs on the <img> SanityHero emits
          rather than on the <figure> around it. It is inside `h-entry` and
          OUTSIDE `e-content`, which is what makes it a photo OF the post rather
          than an image IN the body — a syndicated copy attaches it, and a card
          preview uses it.

          Same property name means something different on an h-card, where
          `u-photo` is the person's avatar. Different root, different subject.
        */}
        <SanityHero image={hero} class="u-photo" />
        {hero.caption && <figcaption>{hero.caption}</figcaption>}
      </figure>
    )
  }
</div>

<style>
  /*
   * One axis is all this has to express, so grid's second dimension would be unused
   * machinery. <Grid> handles the horizontal, inside the band.
   *
   * DESIGN.md's "the page stack is a flex column" is still the right rule; this is a
   * note about WHICH stack owns the reorder — see the header.
   */
  .entry-header {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
  }

  /*
   * The whole reorder. At lg the hero sits directly under the masthead; below it, it
   * sits between the title and the prose, which is the DOM order.
   *
   * ON THE <figure>, NOT THE <img>. The caption travels with the picture, so the flex
   * item that moves has to be the thing that holds both — ordering the image alone
   * would leave its credit stranded below the title.
   *
   * The DOM is authored NARROW-FIRST — title, then hero — the rule the footer already
   * follows: source order is reading order, and the wide layout is the exception.
   * `order` is safe only because the hero is a non-interactive image, so nothing
   * focusable changes place. A band with controls must not be reordered this way.
   */
  @container style(--breakpoint-lg: true) {
    .hero-figure {
      order: -1;
    }
  }

  /* ── Title band ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /*
   * `rhythm-heading-close` for the bottom rather than a typed 16, and the NAME is the
   * reason: the title is bound to the paragraph that follows it exactly the way a
   * heading is bound to its prose. That also settles what follows an h1, which the
   * rhythm ramp in base.css never covered — it handles h2-h4 and leaves h1 out.
   *
   * See the header for why the no-hero value is the base and `[data-hero]` opens the
   * gap back up. The two numbers are read off the boards: the Article board draws
   * 64/16 at 1440 and "Insight Detail — Mobile" draws 32/32 at 375, both of which
   * `--rhythm-band`'s own 32 -> 64 clamp supplies without a breakpoint.
   */
  .title-grid {
    padding-block-start: var(--rhythm-band);
    padding-block-end: var(--rhythm-heading-close);
  }

  .entry-header[data-hero] .title-grid {
    padding-block-end: var(--rhythm-band);
  }

  @container style(--breakpoint-lg: true) {
    .entry-header[data-hero] .title-grid {
      padding-block-end: var(--rhythm-heading-close);
    }
  }

  /*
   * One wrapper rather than two grid children. <Grid>'s row gap is
   * `rhythm-heading-major` (64), so an eyebrow and an h1 placed as siblings on the
   * grid would sit 64 apart with no way to say 8 — the gap between them is inside a
   * block, not between grid rows.
   */
  .title-block {
    grid-column: var(--col-main);
  }

  .title-block h1 {
    margin-top: var(--rhythm-tight);
  }

  /* ── The credit hangs off the image's corner ─────────────────────────────── */
  /*
   * From md, right-aligned against the VIEWPORT edge rather than the content column,
   * which is what the live site does and what a photo credit wants: it belongs to the
   * picture, not to the prose. base.css supplies the `caption` role — Noto Serif
   * italic at the 16px step in `text-muted` — and the 8px above it.
   *
   * `--space-2` rather than `--chrome-inset`, which is the token that otherwise means
   * "distance from the viewport edge". `chrome-inset` steps to 48 at md, and 48px of
   * clearance reads as pulled INTO the page — the opposite of the intent. 16 at every
   * width keeps it hugging the corner. Symmetric so a caption long enough to wrap
   * cannot touch the left edge either.
   *
   * ── AND IT GOES LEFT ONCE THE RAIL IS GONE ─────────────────────────────────
   *
   * Below md the page has one column and everything in it is left-aligned, so a
   * right-hung credit is the only thing on the page pulled to that edge and reads as
   * a mistake (Andy, on screen, 2026-08-26). It hangs off a corner only while there
   * is a layout with corners.
   *
   * The left edge lands on the prose without being told to: `--grid-margin` floors at
   * 16px for any viewport under ~1028, and `--space-2` is 16, so below md the two
   * agree by construction rather than by coincidence. They part company above that —
   * which is fine, because that is exactly where this switches to `end`.
   */
  .hero-figure figcaption {
    padding-inline: var(--space-2);
    text-align: start;
  }

  @container style(--breakpoint-md: true) {
    .hero-figure figcaption {
      text-align: end;
    }
  }

  /*
   * ── AND AT lg IT COSTS NO VERTICAL SPACE ───────────────────────────────────
   *
   * THE REQUIREMENT: the page title sits at the same height whether a hero has a
   * caption or not, so someone moving between articles does not see it jump. 19 of 42
   * heroes have one, so in flow it would move the title on nearly half the pages.
   *
   * Taking it out of flow is the only way to say that — hence `position: absolute`,
   * with `.hero-figure` as the containing block. `inset-block-start: 100%` puts it at
   * the image's bottom edge and base.css's `margin-top: var(--rhythm-tight)` still
   * applies as an offset, so it lands 8px below.
   *
   * WHERE IT LANDS is the title band's top padding — 64px of it, against a ~24px
   * line. That is why this works rather than colliding: the caption sits in space the
   * title band was already reserving. It also means the title band MUST NOT GAIN A
   * `tone`: Band paints tone as a background on the band itself, which would be
   * painted over the caption. Nothing here can catch that.
   *
   * ── lg ONLY, AND THE REASON IS THE ORDER SWAP ──────────────────────────────
   *
   * Below lg the title is ALREADY above the hero, so a caption in flow has nothing to
   * push — the requirement cannot be violated there, and in-flow is the simpler,
   * safer thing. Only at lg does the caption sit between the hero and the title. So
   * this is not "desktop gets the fancy version"; it is the one arrangement where the
   * problem exists.
   */
  @container style(--breakpoint-lg: true) {
    .hero-figure {
      position: relative;
    }

    .hero-figure figcaption {
      position: absolute;
      inset-block-start: 100%;
      inset-inline-end: 0;
    }
  }
</style>
